<title>KENYA: KENYA TEA AUCTION QUIET DESPITE BRITISH INTEREST.</title>
<headline>KENYA TEA AUCTION QUIET DESPITE BRITISH INTEREST.</headline>
<dateline>MOMBASA, Kenya 1997-08-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Mombasa tea market was quiet despite overall strong support from Britain and Pakistan buyers, auction officials said on Monday.</p>
<p>They said there was maintained interest from Eygpt and other Middle Eastern countries, but less interest from the Sudan.</p>
<p>Brighter BP1s were steady to three U.S. cents cheaper, while mediums gained 3-7 cents. Lower mediums were firm to dearer.</p>
<p>Plainer BP1s were discounted by up to five cents. Brighter PF1s were irregular but were mostly firm to two cents dearer.</p>
<p>--Matthew Green, Nairobi Newsroom, ++254-2 330-261</p>
</text>
